  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275039/assets/7147abe07e836df2366f3445c455162499cfb0ac320ee662d60b070268e0458b.png)\n\nFind the $y$-intercepts of the circle $(x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=20,$ shown above.",
  "worked_solution": "We must have $x=0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =20 \\\\ (0-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =20 \\\\ 4+(y-2)^{2} & =20 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ y-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{16} \\\\ y & =2\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts occur at the points $(0,-2)$ and $(0,6).$",
